		<!-- Acknowledgements and the name -->
		<section class="kk-section kk-ack" aria-labelledby="ack-title">
			<div class="kk-container">
				<div class="kk-section__head">
					<h2 id="ack-title" class="kk-section__title">Built on Percona, RabbitMQ, Elastic, Supabase and other open-source engines, inspired by OpenEverest</h2>
				</div>
				<div class="kk-ack__grid">
					<article class="kk-ack__item">
						<h3>Percona</h3>
						<p>
							Thank you to Percona for <a href="https://www.percona.com/mongodb/software/percona-server-for-mongodb" rel="noopener">Percona Server for MongoDB</a>, <a
								href="https://docs.percona.com/percona-backup-mongodb/latest/"
								rel="noopener">Percona Backup for MongoDB</a>, <a href="https://www.percona.com/postgresql/software" rel="noopener">Percona Distribution for PostgreSQL</a>, <a
								href="https://www.percona.com/mysql/software/percona-server-for-mysql"
								rel="noopener">Percona Server for MySQL</a>, <a href="https://www.percona.com/mysql/software/percona-xtradb-cluster" rel="noopener">Percona XtraDB Cluster</a> and <a
								href="https://www.percona.com/mysql/software/percona-xtrabackup"
								rel="noopener">Percona XtraBackup</a>.
							Karakoram installs the MongoDB and PostgreSQL packages from Percona's official repositories at <a href="https://repo.percona.com/" rel="noopener">repo.percona.com</a> and the MySQL family from Percona's official checksum-locked tarballs.
							The engines, replication, backups and point-in-time recovery come from their open-source work, together with <a
								href="https://patroni.readthedocs.io"
								rel="noopener">Patroni</a>, <a href="https://etcd.io" rel="noopener">etcd</a>, <a href="https://pgbackrest.org" rel="noopener">pgBackRest</a>, <a
								href="https://proxysql.com"
								rel="noopener">ProxySQL</a> and, for PostgreSQL profiles and the distributed topology, <a href="https://postgis.net" rel="noopener">PostGIS</a>, <a
								href="https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"
								rel="noopener">pgvector</a>, <a href="https://www.timescale.com" rel="noopener">TimescaleDB</a> (Apache-only build) and <a
								href="https://github.com/citusdata/citus"
								rel="noopener">Citus</a> (AGPL-3.0, downloaded separately, never bundled); Karakoram coordinates those capabilities rather than reinventing them.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="kk-ack__item">
						<h3>RabbitMQ and Elastic</h3>
						<p>
							Thank you to the <a href="https://www.rabbitmq.com" rel="noopener">RabbitMQ</a> team for the broker, quorum queues and the
							Management API, and to <a href="https://www.elastic.co" rel="noopener">Elastic</a> for Elasticsearch, Kibana, Logstash and APM Server.
							Karakoram installs both on your own runtimes at deploy time, from RabbitMQ's package repositories and Elastic's official
							distributions, and redistributes no binaries. Elastic's default distributions carry their own license terms, described in
							the <a href={docs('elasticsearch/#licensing')}>Elasticsearch guide</a>.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="kk-ack__item">
						<h3>Supabase</h3>
						<p>
							Thank you to <a href="https://supabase.com" rel="noopener">Supabase</a> for Auth, PostgREST, Realtime, Storage, Studio, Edge Runtime,
							Supavisor and the rest of the open-source stack that the Supabase engine assembles on top of the PostgreSQL engine, and to <a
								href="https://www.envoyproxy.io"
								rel="noopener">Envoy</a> for the gateway. Component versions are locked to each Karakoram release and installed on your own
							runtimes from checksum-pinned artifacts; see <a href={docs('supabase/components/')}>components and availability</a>.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="kk-ack__item">
						<h3>Weaviate and Neo4j</h3>
						<p>
							Thank you to <a href="https://weaviate.io" rel="noopener">Weaviate</a> for the vector database, used with community features only
							under its mixed-license binary boundary, and to <a href="https://neo4j.com" rel="noopener">Neo4j</a> for Neo4j Community (GPLv3) and
							the graph tooling around it. Both are downloaded as official, checksum-locked artifacts onto your own runtimes; Karakoram redistributes
							no binaries. See <a href={docs('native-artifacts/')}>native artifacts</a> for provenance and license notices.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="kk-ack__item">
						<h3>Keycloak, OpenBao and Temporal</h3>
						<p>
							Thank you to <a href="https://www.keycloak.org" rel="noopener">Keycloak</a> for the identity server, to <a
								href="https://openbao.org"
								rel="noopener">OpenBao</a> (MPL-2.0) for the secrets manager with integrated Raft, and to <a href="https://temporal.io" rel="noopener">Temporal</a> for the workflow
							server and its web UI. Keycloak and Temporal keep their state in a PostgreSQL cluster that Karakoram binds to them; OpenBao owns its own
							Raft volumes. Each runs from release-locked artifacts on runtimes you provide.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="kk-ack__item">
						<h3>OpenEverest</h3>
						<p>
							<a href="https://openeverest.io" rel="noopener">OpenEverest</a> is the inspiration for this project. Originally Percona Everest,
							it is an Apache-2.0 CNCF Sandbox project whose model of self-hosted, provider-based database orchestration shaped Karakoram:
							Provider and Instance concepts, version bundles, topology schemas, backup storages, PITR and restore flows, and RBAC.
							Karakoram brings that idea to platforms such as Zerops, Dokploy and Nomad rather than Kubernetes. It is an independent implementation;
							no OpenEverest code or UI is copied.
						</p>
					</article>
					<article class="kk-ack__item kk-ack__item--name">
						<h3>The name</h3>
						<p class="kk-ack__quote">
							Everest is the highest peak. Karakoram is the range that holds K2 and other giants.
						</p>
						<p>
							The name is a nod to OpenEverest and to growing beyond a single peak. Karakoram orchestrates databases and stateful services
							on Zerops, Dokploy and Nomad; see the engine guides for backend availability.
							More engines and backends are a direction, not a claim that they exist.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

